%I A399577 #43 Sep 17 2026 04:38:40
%S A399577 7,10,13,23,31,32,44,49,70,79,94,97,103,130,203,226,230,236,239,262,
%T A399577 263,293,301,302,310,320,326,329,356,362,365,367,376,392,404,409,440,
%U A399577 490,536,563,566,622,623,632,635,637,653,656,665,673,700,709,736,763,790,899
%N A399577 Happy numbers whose trajectories under iteration of the sum of squares of digits map to 10 before going to the value of 1.
%C A399577 Powers of 10 are the only numbers that map to 1 in one iteration of the sum of squares of digits mapping. [Edited by _Jianing Song_, Sep 09 2026]
%C A399577 Infinite since it contains the subfamily 13*10^k for k >= 0 (among all numbers with one 1, one 3, and all other digits 0). - _Michael S. Branicky_, Sep 06 2026
%H A399577 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A399577/b399577.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399577 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happy_number">Happy number</a>.
%e A399577 13 is a term since 13 -> 1^2 + 3^2 = 10 -> 1^2 + 0^2 = 1.
%e A399577 28 is not a term since 28 -> 2^2 + 8^2 = 68 -> 6^2 + 8^2 = 100 -> 1^2 + 0^2 + 0^2 = 1.
%e A399577 2 is not a term since it is not happy (cf. A000216).
%t A399577 q[n_] := MemberQ[NestWhileList[Plus @@ (IntegerDigits[#]^2) &, n, UnsameQ, All], 10]; Select[Range[1000], q] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 04 2026 *)
%o A399577 (PARI) isA399577(n) = while(n!=1 && n!=4 && n!=10, n=A003132(n)); return(n==10) \\ _Jianing Song_, Sep 06 2026; see A003132 for its program
%Y A399577 Subsequence of A007770.
%Y A399577 Cf. A000216, A003132.
%K A399577 nonn,base,easy,new
%O A399577 1,1
%A A399577 _Harry E. Neel_, Sep 03 2026
